{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>ראשית חכמה קנה חכמה ובכל קנינך קנה בינה (משלי ד, ז)</b>" "<b>King Solomon</b>, peace be upon him, taught the people knowledge in this verse (Mishlei 4): that they should acquire the wisdom of the Torah first, before the other wisdoms. And he said: at the beginning of the other wisdoms, acquire the wisdom of the Torah — this is what he said, ״ראשית חכמה ״ and so forth, ״ובכל קנינך קנה בינה״: after a person has acquired this wisdom, he will still need to acquire understanding (בינה), so that he may understand one matter from within another matter, for the perfection of wisdom is not complete without understanding. And because understanding is the perfection of the wisdoms, therefore he said that a person should give all the wealth of his house for understanding — this is ״ובכל קנינך קנה בינה״, that is: even if you give all your money for it, acquire it, for it is the true wealth. And so Wisdom cries out and says (Mishlei 8): ״עושר וכבוד אתי הון עתק וצדקה״.\n\nAnd King Solomon, peace be upon him, needed to inform us here that a person must set aside for himself the wisdom of the entire Torah first, before the other wisdoms, because if he engages in the other wisdoms first, they will turn his heart away from the path of wisdom and produce in him corrupt opinions. But when he engages in the wisdom of the Torah first, making it his root and the foundation of his beginning, then he is fit to build upon that foundation the structure of the other wisdoms, which will no longer have the power to corrupt his opinion or confound his thought, because the wisdom of the Torah is likened to refined silver. And so David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 12:7): ״אמרות ה׳ אמרות טהורות כסף צרוף בעליל לארץ מזוקק שבעתים״ — according to this explanation, for all the other wisdoms are indeed silver, but not refined silver, because they contain dross and admixtures and it is possible that they may bring a person to the point of corrupting faith; but our Torah is without dross, like refined silver of the lord of the land, from which he makes vessels for his food or his drink, containing no dross or admixture whatsoever.\n\nAnd upon this King Solomon, peace be upon him, said here: ״ראשית חכמה קנה חכמה״ — for if he did not study the wisdom of the Torah first and did not see in the Torah the explanation of the signs and wonders and great and well-known miracles, it is likely that he will be drawn after nature and believe in the eternity of the universe. For this is why nature (טבע) is called by this name: because a person will sink (יטבע) in the depth of its waters and descend into the pit of destruction if he does not take care regarding it, like one who comes into the depths of the sea and does not know how to swim and drowns. So too a person is likely to doubt, through the wisdom of nature, the signs and wonders that were performed for Israel through Moses our teacher, peace be upon him, and to believe only in natural things that are perceptible to the eye; and he will go astray in the path of faith, saying of the miracles of the wilderness that they were natural events with nothing miraculous in them, and he will say that the wilderness where Israel stood for forty years was like the other wildernesses that are near settled areas — like those deserts in which Arabs dwell, where it is possible for a person to plow and sow and sustain himself from the fruit of trees and herbage that are there, as the Rambam, of blessed memory, wrote in the Guide for the Perplexed — or he will have doubts and say: perhaps there were many wells of water there.\n\nAnd in order to uproot the root of this corrupt opinion and to establish in the heart faith in those great wonders, the wisdom of the Torah came, requiring a person to give it priority over the other wisdoms, and it illuminated our eyes and informed us that that wilderness was not like the other wildernesses. As it is written (Deuteronomy 8:15): ״המוליכך במדבר הגדול והנורא נחש שרף ועקרב״ — and it was not near settled land that could receive plowing and sowing and the work of growth, as it is written (Numbers 20:5): ״לא מקום זרע ותאנה וגפן ורמון״; and there was no water there at all, as it is written (Deuteronomy 8:15): ״וצמאון אשר אין מים״, (Numbers 20:5): ״ומים אין לשתות״. And furthermore, it was not in the nature of man to live there even a single day — all the more so with the weight of men, women, and children who stood there for forty years. And of this Scripture says (Jeremiah 2:6): ״ארץ לא עבר בה איש ולא ישב אדם שם״ — that is, it is not in human nature to live there.\n\nAnd in order to strengthen this faith in the hearts, the Torah came and elaborated in recounting the journeys that they made, which were in total forty-two journeys, and it wished to mention them by name, for there are many benefits in their recounting, for that hour and for all generations: that the great wonder might become known for that hour among the nations of that generation who heard the report of Egypt and knew some of the places where it is not in human nature to live; and furthermore, that it might become known also for the coming generations who did not see the event, and that the faith might be fixed in their hearts — that those generations which saw it would tell this to their children, and their children to their children, and their children to the last generation, how Israel stood there not by way of nature but by way of miracle. And so too all their deeds were by way of miracle, according to the matter and through the two redeemers, Moses and Aaron. And this is what is written:" "<b>These are the journeys of the children of Israel who went out of the land of Egypt by their hosts, by the hand of Moses and Aaron.</b>"], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:37:48.647205532Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}